sections.s2_technical:
Upload and transcoding form an asynchronous pipeline. The raw upload lands in object storage, which emits an event that queues transcoding jobs. Workers transcode the video into a ladder of resolutions and bitrates and split each into short segments, typically a few seconds each, producing a manifest that lists the available quality levels. This work is slow and bursty, so it must be decoupled from the upload request through a queue.

```java
// Upload triggers an async transcoding job per quality level
public void onUpload(String videoId, String rawPath) {
    for (String profile : List.of("240p", "480p", "720p", "1080p"))
        transcodeQueue.publish(new TranscodeJob(videoId, rawPath, profile));
}
```

Delivery uses a content delivery network. The transcoded segments are pushed to edge servers around the world so a viewer downloads from a nearby edge rather than the distant origin, which cuts latency and offloads the origin. Adaptive bitrate streaming, via HLS or DASH, means the player downloads a manifest listing all quality levels and then requests the segments whose bitrate matches the current measured bandwidth, switching up or down seamlessly as conditions change.

```java
// Player picks the highest bitrate that fits current bandwidth
public String selectVariant(List<Variant> ladder, long bandwidthBps) {
    return ladder.stream()
        .filter(v -> v.bitrate() <= bandwidthBps)
        .max(Comparator.comparingLong(Variant::bitrate))
        .map(Variant::url).orElse(ladder.get(0).url()); // fall back to lowest
}
```

The recommendation engine analyzes viewing history, watch time, and similarity between videos and users to suggest what to watch next, usually precomputed offline and served from a fast store. View counting at scale cannot use a single exact counter per video because popular videos receive enormous concurrent increments; instead, counts are aggregated approximately and asynchronously, often with sharded counters or probabilistic structures, and are eventually consistent. A senior nuance is that playback and segment delivery demand low latency and high availability but tolerate eventual consistency in metadata like view counts, so the design deliberately separates the strongly consistent control plane from the eventually consistent, high-throughput data plane.

